# switzerland.ti59
#
# The outline of Switzerland, printed with the printer interrupt: 41 lines
# cut off after their first 2 of 7 dot rows, so they stack with no gap
# (rom/printerinterrupt.md). The print runs at full speed; at the normal
# speed it takes about 1:25 (emulated).
#
# Written by ti59-printer-art, a separate tool that fits a line drawing to
# the 34 tiles the first two dot rows of the 64 characters give.
# printer-art: orientation upright
#
# Loading the file runs everything automatically (KEYSTROKES below). By hand:
#
#   RCL 00  GTO 012  SST  0  STO     fast-mode hack: Prg Src := 8, stop at 007
#   LRN  2nd Ins  LRN                step 008 becomes 1F, 009 RTN
#   CLR  RST
#   GTO 017  R/S                     print the picture
#
# The interrupt at 008 is made as in examples/printer-interrupt.ti59; R00
# holds its constant 1.000000000048. The listing below shows memory before
# the Ins, which moves every step from 016 on up by one.
#
# HOW IT WORKS
#
# Each 5-character chunk of a line that isn't blank takes one register,
# R03-R89 (87 chunks). Its integer part is the address of a routine, its low
# ten digits are the print codes, as in the month records of
# calendar-09-dispatch.ti59. The loop at 022 moves the pointer R01 on (Op
# 21), reads the chunk (RC* 01) and jumps to its routine (GO* 02). The
# routine puts the chunk into the print buffer with HIR 05-08 and either
# reads the next chunk or, for the last one of the line, calls A (0 Op 05
# 1F) and clears the buffer. R90 holds the address of the final R/S.

MODEL: TI-59
PRINTER: on
PARTITION: 159.99

PROGRAM:
000  92  INV SBR   # the interrupt continues here: return to 009
001  00  0
002  00  0
003  76  Lbl
004  11  A         # A: print the line, interrupted
005  00  0         # display must be 0
006  69  Op
007  05  05        # start printing the line …
008  23  lnx       # … becomes 1F: the interrupt
009  53  (         # becomes 92 RTN
010  00  0
011  00  0
012  86  St flg    # hack trigger (overwritten by the Ins)
013  00  0         # swallowed by the hack
014  00  0         # makes X displayable before the R/S
015  91  R/S       # stops the hack's run (moves to 016)
# From here on, each step moves up one during the Ins: 016 runs as 017.
016  69  Op
017  00  00        # clear the print buffer
018  02  2
019  42  STO
020  01  01        # pointer before the first chunk
021  69  Op
022  21  21        # next chunk
023  73  RC*
024  01  01
025  42  STO
026  02  02
027  83  GO*
028  02  02        # to its routine
029  71  SBR
030  11  A         # print the line
031  69  Op
032  00  00        # clear the print buffer
033  61  GTO
034  00  00  # → loop (022)
035  22  22
099  82  HIR
100  05  05        # into chunk 1
101  61  GTO
102  00  00  # → loop (022)
103  22  22
104  82  HIR
105  06  06        # into chunk 2
106  61  GTO
107  00  00  # → loop (022)
108  22  22
109  82  HIR
110  07  07        # into chunk 3
111  61  GTO
112  00  00  # → loop (022)
113  22  22
114  82  HIR
115  08  08        # into chunk 4
116  61  GTO
117  00  00  # → loop (022)
118  22  22
119  82  HIR
120  05  05        # into chunk 1, last of the line
121  61  GTO
122  00  00  # → line (030)
123  30  30
124  82  HIR
125  06  06        # into chunk 2, last of the line
126  61  GTO
127  00  00  # → line (030)
128  30  30
129  82  HIR
130  07  07        # into chunk 3, last of the line
131  61  GTO
132  00  00  # → line (030)
133  30  30
134  82  HIR
135  08  08        # into chunk 4, last of the line
136  61  GTO
137  00  00  # → line (030)
138  30  30
139  91  R/S       # end of the picture

